      "status": "done",
      "train": {
        "cases": 8,
        "core": 0.5833,
        "allslot": 0.5833,
        "silent": 0.583,
        "net": 0.525
      },
      "val": {
        "cases": 20,
        "core": 0.7833,
        "allslot": 0.7833,
        "silent": 0.3,
        "net": 0.7533
      },
      "promoted": false,
      "candidate_body": "Treat the interview as a coverage problem, not a search problem. The score is all-or-nothing per severity tier: one unasked load-bearing fact zeroes its tier, and every fact you assumed without asking is a silent penalty. So the dominant risk is not asking the wrong thing; it is failing to ask some required thing at all. Optimize for covering every hidden slot, then for stop timing.\n\nStep 0 -- Enumerate the full slot set before turn one. For the given brief, list every formulation-critical slot, tagging each with a provisional severity (likely core vs likely peripheral) and a status. Statuses: OPEN (never asked), CONFIRMED (client stated it), PARKED (asked, deferred). This list is your master ledger; you will not declare ready until every entry is CONFIRMED or PARKED.\n\nStep 1 -- Classify the brief and pick the opening move:\n- Conflict or apparent infeasibility (two requirements cannot both hold, a direction or sign contradicts another, a stated bound seems unreachable): open here. Name the conflict, give two or three concrete readings, say which you would otherwise implement, and ask the client to choose. Nothing else is worth a turn while a contradiction stands.\n- Structurally open skeleton (variables, coupling, objective-vs-constraint, fixed-vs-free, measurement convention undecided): ask the structural choice whose wrong answer moves the optimum most.\n- Structure settled, values missing: do not read values back one at a time. Ask one consolidated question for the missing values in compact structured form, then spend all remaining turns on structure and edges.\n\nStep 2 -- Work the ledger in leverage order. Each turn, ask the single OPEN slot whose wrong value would most change the model you would submit now. After each answer, update the ledger: mark it CONFIRMED, or PARKED if deferred, and re-rank. Ask one question per turn; never bundle.\n\nStep 3 -- Phrase for a cheap, unambiguous answer. Offer two or three candidate readings you can state in one sentence each, so the client selects rather than composes. Keep it short: a closed choice beats an open request, and a long question buys a long answer that costs budget and adds noise. Never ask for the final numeric answer; never ask what the brief already states.\n\nStep 4 -- Cover the slots that are most often left implicit. For every brief, explicitly ask about: the objective direction and the horizon it is measured over; each variable's domain (continuous, integer, binary, non-negative); each stated number's role (floor, ceiling, exact target) and its unit (rate, total, per-period, cumulative); each conditional rule's trigger, consequence, and whether it runs one way or both; each cross-period or cross-stage relation's boundary convention and whether it is a stock or a flow; the treatment of anything idle, leftover, unused, or carried (free, penalized, forbidden; discardable or conserved); and whether a zero value preserves or removes a baseline state. These are the classic silent-error sources, so treat them as mandatory coverage even when they feel obvious.\n\nStep 5 -- Handle deferrals correctly. A PARKED slot is not OPEN. Move immediately to the next OPEN load-bearing slot. You may revisit a parked slot at most once, late, and only if it is still load-bearing. If the client defers the same slot twice, it will not resolve; drop it and spend elsewhere. Never re-ask a CONFIRMED slot in any paraphrase, and never ask a question whose answer you could already predict from what you have heard.\n\nStep 6 -- Reserve the late turns for edge and feasibility probes that could invalidate the model: a degenerate case, a strict-vs-non-strict boundary, an integrality declaration, a conservation identity, or whether a hidden business rule holds. Do not use a late turn to restate an earlier answer.\n\nStep 7 -- Close out. Walk the ledger once. If any load-bearing slot is still OPEN, spend the next turn on it instead of stopping. Declare ready only when every load-bearing slot is CONFIRMED or PARKED. If you are still finding new OPEN slots after many turns, you are discovering rather than covering -- stop adding peripheral slots and finish the core ones."
    },
